// Generated by tools/build-content.mjs. Edit the Markdown in content/ instead.
export default {"basecamp/ai-era":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two engineers, one assistant","md":"During a Friday incident, a disk filled up on a payment server. Engineer A pasted the alert into an AI chat, got back `sudo rm -rf /var/log/*`, and ran it. The disk recovered. So did the attackers who had been in the system for a week: the logs that would have shown them were gone, and so was the audit trail the company needed for its compliance report.\n\nEngineer B, on another team, used the same assistant differently. She asked it to explain an unfamiliar error, to draft a runbook from her rough notes, and to suggest *where to look*. She read every suggested command, asked \"what does `-f` do here?\", tried it on staging first, and kept a human decision at every step. Her team's time to recover from incidents dropped by a third.\n\nSame tool. The difference was the person's judgment."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What AI changes, and what it does not","md":"AI assistants (chat models, code copilots, and increasingly **agents** that can run tools) are now part of everyday DevOps work. They are good at some things and dangerous at others.\n\n| AI is strong at | AI is weak at |\n|---|---|\n| Explaining commands, errors and concepts | Knowing *your* systems, history and constraints |\n| Drafting scripts, configs, docs and tests | Recent changes, versions and your company's rules |\n| Summarising long logs, threads and diffs | Knowing when it is wrong (it sounds equally sure either way) |\n| Suggesting where to look next | Understanding side effects and blast radius |\n\nThe failure mode has a name: **hallucination**, a confident answer that is simply invented, such as a flag that does not exist or an API removed two versions ago.\n\n```diagram\nai-verify\n```\n\nThe rule for the rest of this course: **the AI drafts, you decide**. Every suggestion goes through the same loop: read it, understand each part, test it somewhere safe, apply it in a small step, and observe the result.\n\nWhat does not change: you still need the fundamentals. You cannot verify a command you do not understand, and you cannot debug at 3 a.m. with an assistant if you cannot tell a good answer from a bad one. That is why this course teaches the fundamentals first and the AI-assisted workflows on top."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The brilliant new intern","md":"Imagine an intern who has read every manual ever written, answers instantly and never gets tired, but joined this morning, has never seen your systems, and sometimes makes things up rather than say \"I don't know\". You would happily ask them for drafts and ideas. You would not hand them the production root password and go to lunch."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"How good teams use AI","md":"- **No secrets in prompts.** No passwords, keys, tokens or customer data in a chat window.\n- **Review AI output like a junior colleague's pull request**: line by line, with tests.\n- **Humans approve actions.** Agents that run commands get the least privilege possible, a dry-run mode and a human approval step for anything risky.\n- **Keep an audit trail** of what AI tools did and who approved it.\n- **Use AI to learn, not to skip learning.** Ask it *why*, not just *what*."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"AI traps","md":"**Copy, paste, enter.** Running a command you cannot explain on a real system.\n\n**Confidence equals correctness.** The tone of an answer tells you nothing about whether it is right.\n\n**Fixing the symptom.** \"Delete the logs\" makes the alert go away; it does not answer why the disk filled up.\n\n**Over-sharing.** Pasting a config file with database passwords into a public AI tool is a security incident."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Your new AI pair","md":"Your laptop's disk is almost full and builds are failing. The terminal has a simulated AI assistant: type `ai \"your question\"` to ask it, and `ai explain \"<command>\"` to have any command broken down with a risk rating.\n\nUse it the way Engineer B would: as a helper whose suggestions you check."}},"basecamp/basecamp-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Your first day at Lumen","md":"It is your first morning on Lumen's platform team. You have barely set up your laptop when the team chat lights up: **\"Checkout feels broken, can someone look?\"** Everyone senior is in a planning meeting.\n\nYou will not fix the problem today. Your job is to **investigate calmly and report clearly**: what is broken, what the evidence says, and what you think the next step is. That is the most valuable thing a new person can do in an incident."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Base Camp gave you","md":"- **DevOps** is shared ownership of the whole loop, with automation and fast feedback; CALMS and the Three Ways describe it, and the four DORA metrics measure it.\n- Delivery is a **loop** and a **value stream**: most lead time is waiting, so shrink queues with small batches, less work in progress and automation.\n- **AI assistants** draft, explain and summarise; you read, verify, test safely and decide.\n- Your first terminal habits: `pwd`, `ls`, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `grep`, `wc`, `curl`, and `echo … >> file`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Checkout feels broken","md":"Investigate the report. Lumen's public status API is at `https://status.lumen.example.com/api`, and the team keeps its runbooks somewhere in your home folder. Collect evidence and write a short incident note in `~/incident.md`."}},"basecamp/delivery-loop":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Forty-seven days for one button","md":"Lumen's product manager asked a simple question: \"Why did the *Add to calendar* button take 47 days to reach users? It was two days of coding.\"\n\nThe team traced the button's journey through every system it touched. Coding: 2 days. Waiting for a code review: 6 days. Waiting for the next test cycle: 9 days. Manual testing: 3 days. Waiting for the monthly release window: 22 days. Release and verification: 5 days.\n\nOnly **10 of the 47 days** involved anyone working on it. The rest was **waiting** in queues between teams. Making developers type faster would have changed almost nothing. Shrinking the queues changed everything: smaller pull requests got reviewed the same day, automated tests replaced the test cycle, and continuous delivery removed the release window. The next button took four days."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The loop and the value stream","md":"Software delivery is a loop, not a line. What you learn from running software feeds the next plan.\n\n```diagram\ndevops-loop\n```\n\n| Phase | The question it answers | Typical tools (you will use all of these) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Plan | What should we build next, and why? | Issues, roadmaps |\n| Code | How do we build it together? | Git, pull requests, code review |\n| Build | Can we turn it into something runnable? | Compilers, package managers, Docker |\n| Test | Does it work, and is it safe? | Unit and integration tests, scanners |\n| Release | Is this exact version ready to ship? | Version tags, artifacts, registries |\n| Deploy | How do we put it in front of users safely? | Pipelines, Kubernetes, rollouts |\n| Operate | How do we keep it running? | Linux, systemd, cloud, infrastructure as code |\n| Monitor | Is it healthy? Are users happy? | Metrics, logs, traces, alerts |\n\nA **value stream** is every step from \"someone has an idea\" to \"a user benefits from it\". Two numbers matter:\n\n- **Lead time**: how long the whole journey takes.\n- **Process time**: how much of that someone actually worked on it.\n\nThe ratio between them, the *flow efficiency*, is usually shocking: 10 to 20% is common. The biggest wins come from removing waiting, not from working faster.\n\nThree levers shrink waiting:\n\n1. **Smaller batches**: small changes are reviewed, tested and deployed faster, and are easier to debug.\n2. **Less work in progress**: finishing beats starting. Ten half-done things deliver nothing.\n3. **Automation of handoffs**: a pipeline does not have a queue of tickets."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The coffee shop queue","md":"A coffee shop can have the fastest barista in the city, but if every order first waits at a cashier who takes payments one at a time, then for a manager to approve oat milk, the line still goes out the door. You speed the shop up by removing the waits between people, not by asking the barista to move faster."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Reading the evidence","md":"DevOps work starts with evidence, and evidence usually lives in text files: logs, exports, reports. A handful of commands go a long way:\n\n```term\n$ cat feature-4521.log          # print the whole file\n$ head -n 3 feature-4521.log    # just the first 3 lines\n$ tail -n 2 feature-4521.log    # just the last 2 lines\n$ wc -l feature-4521.log        # count the lines\n$ grep -i wait feature-4521.log # only lines containing \"wait\" (any case)\n$ echo \"note\" >> findings.txt   # append a line to a file\n```\n\nYou will learn these properly in the Linux realm; here they help you investigate."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Making flow visible","md":"Teams that improve delivery make the work visible:\n\n- A board where every item shows its stage, and how long it has waited there.\n- A weekly look at the DORA metrics, not to blame anyone but to spot the biggest queue.\n- **Work-in-progress limits**: \"no more than three items in review at once\" forces the team to finish reviews before starting new work.\n- Retrospectives that ask \"where did this wait?\" rather than \"who was slow?\"."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Flow traps","md":"**Optimising one step.** Making builds two minutes faster does not help if changes wait two weeks for a release window.\n\n**Big batches \"to save time\".** One release with 200 changes saves ceremony and costs you a week of debugging when it breaks.\n\n**Measuring people instead of flow.** Lines of code or tickets closed per person encourage the wrong behaviour. Measure the system."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Follow the button","md":"Lumen's tracking system exported the journey of feature **#4521** (the *Add to calendar* button) as a log file in your home folder. Investigate where the time went, and write your findings to a file."}},"basecamp/what-is-devops":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two releases a year","md":"In 2019, Lumen Tickets shipped software twice a year. Developers spent five months writing features, then handed a 400 MB zip file to the operations team with a 30-page installation guide. Release weekend meant pizza, panic and a 2 a.m. call: the new version needed a library the servers did not have, and nobody on the ops side knew which feature was causing the database to melt.\n\nDevelopers were rewarded for shipping features. Operations was rewarded for stability. So developers pushed for change and operations pushed back against it. Both were doing their jobs, and the company lost either way.\n\nFive years later, the same company deploys about 30 times a day. Each change is small, tested automatically, rolled out gradually and watched closely. When something breaks, the developer who wrote it is paged, has a dashboard showing what changed, and can roll back in one command. Outages still happen, but they are smaller, shorter and less frightening.\n\nThey did not just buy new tools. They changed **who owns what**, **how work flows**, and **how fast they learn**. That change is what people mean by **DevOps**."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What DevOps is (and is not)","md":"**DevOps** is a way of building and running software in which the same team owns the whole journey from idea to production, and uses automation and fast feedback to make that journey safe, quick and repeatable.\n\n```diagram\nwall-of-confusion\n```\n\nA useful checklist is **CALMS**:\n\n| Pillar | Means | Looks like |\n|---|---|---|\n| **C**ulture | Shared ownership, no blame | \"You build it, you run it\"; blameless postmortems |\n| **A**utomation | Machines do the repetitive, error-prone steps | Pipelines, infrastructure as code, auto-rollbacks |\n| **L**ean | Small batches, limit waiting, remove waste | Small pull requests, deploys several times a day |\n| **M**easurement | Decide with data | Delivery metrics, dashboards, service-level objectives |\n| **S**haring | Knowledge flows between people and teams | Runbooks, docs, pairing, open postmortems |\n\nThe **Three Ways** describe the direction of travel:\n\n1. **Flow**: make work move quickly and smoothly from idea to user (small batches, automation, fewer handoffs).\n2. **Feedback**: see problems quickly and close to where they started (tests, monitoring, alerts that reach the right people).\n3. **Continuous learning**: experiment, learn from failures, and get better every week.\n\nAnd four numbers, the **DORA metrics**, tell you how well it works:\n\n- **Deployment frequency**: how often you ship.\n- **Lead time for changes**: from commit to running in production.\n- **Change failure rate**: what share of deploys cause a problem.\n- **Time to restore service**: how fast you recover when they do.\n\nElite teams score well on all four at once: speed and stability are not a trade-off."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The pit crew","md":"A Formula 1 pit stop takes about two seconds. Twenty people change four tyres, adjust the wing and send the car out. Nobody hands a form to another department; every person knows the whole sequence, the tools are laid out in advance, every move is rehearsed, and they review the footage afterwards to shave off another tenth.\n\nNow imagine the tyres were changed by a separate company that only hears about the stop when the car arrives, with no rehearsal and a 30-page manual. That is the old dev-and-ops split. DevOps is building the pit crew."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Who does DevOps?","md":"Job titles vary, and they cause confusion:\n\n- **Developers** write features and also tests, deployment configuration and dashboards for what they ship.\n- **Site Reliability Engineers (SRE)** treat operations as a software problem: they set reliability targets, automate toil and lead incident response.\n- **Platform engineers** build the internal \"paved road\": pipelines, templates and self-service tools so product teams can ship safely without being experts in everything.\n- **\"DevOps engineer\"** usually means someone who builds and runs that automation. Useful, as long as it does not become a new wall between dev and ops.\n\nWhatever the title, the skills overlap: Linux, networking, scripting, CI/CD, containers, cloud, observability, security and, increasingly, working well with AI tools. That is exactly the path ahead of you."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Common misunderstandings","md":"**\"DevOps is a tool.\"** Buying Kubernetes does not make you DevOps, just as buying running shoes does not make you a runner.\n\n**\"DevOps means developers do everything alone.\"** It means shared ownership with good tooling, not dumping ops work on developers without support.\n\n**\"DevOps is a team in the middle.\"** A separate \"DevOps team\" that receives tickets from dev and hands work to ops recreates the wall with a new name.\n\n**\"Moving fast means breaking things.\"** Small, automated, observable changes are *safer* than big, rare ones."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"First contact","md":"Welcome to **Lumen Tickets**. This is your laptop: a Linux machine with a terminal. Everything in the course happens in terminals like this one, connected to simulated servers, pipelines and clusters.\n\nYou do not need to know any commands yet. Each task tells you what to type, and the **Hint** and **Show a solution** buttons help when you are stuck. Watch the panels on the right change as you work."}},"linux/files-editors":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The config file that was \"definitely saved\"","md":"Samir changed the API timeout on a staging server from 5 to 30 seconds, closed the editor and restarted the service. Nothing changed. Twenty minutes and two colleagues later, they found it: he had edited `/etc/lumen/api.conf.bak`, a backup someone had left next to the real file. The `.bak` had sorted first in his tab completion.\n\nWorking with files is the bread and butter of operations: config files, logs, scripts, releases. Most mistakes are not exotic. They are the wrong file, the wrong folder, or a delete that took more than intended."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Files, directories and wildcards","md":"- A **file** holds content; a **directory** holds files and other directories.\n- Creating, copying, moving and deleting are all one command each.\n- There is **no recycle bin** on the command line. `rm` is final.\n\n**Wildcards** (globs) let one command act on many files. The *shell* expands them before the command runs:\n\n| Pattern | Matches |\n|---|---|\n| `*.log` | every name ending in `.log` |\n| `report-2026-0?.csv` | `?` is exactly one character |\n| `img[123].png` | one character from the set |\n\nSo `rm *.log` becomes `rm app.log error.log old.log` before `rm` even starts. That is powerful and dangerous: always preview with `ls *.log` first.\n\nTo **read** files: `cat` prints everything, `less` pages through big files (`q` quits, `/word` searches), `head` and `tail` show the start and the end.\n\nTo **edit** files in a terminal, use an editor such as `nano` (friendly) or `vim` (powerful). In this course, `nano file`, `vim file` and `code file` all open an editor panel: type, then **Ctrl+S** saves."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Creating, moving, reading","md":"```term\n$ mkdir -p projects/api/config     # create nested folders (-p: parents too)\n$ touch notes.md                   # create an empty file (or update its time)\n$ cp app.conf app.conf.orig        # copy (make a backup before editing!)\n$ cp -r site/ site-backup/         # copy a directory: -r is recursive\n$ mv draft.md README.md            # rename\n$ mv *.png images/                 # move many files at once\n$ rm old.txt                       # delete a file (no undo)\n$ rm -r build/                     # delete a directory and its contents\n$ head -n 20 app.log               # the first 20 lines\n$ tail -n 50 app.log               # the last 50 lines\n$ less app.log                     # page through it\n$ nano app.conf                    # edit it\n```\n\n```replay\ntitle: Organise before you delete\nscenario: laptop\nsetup: mkdir -p ~/downloads && cd ~/downloads && touch a.log b.log photo.png notes.md\nstep: ls | A messy folder.\nstep: ls *.log | Preview what the pattern matches before acting on it.\nstep: mkdir -p logs && mv *.log logs/ | One command moves every match.\nstep: ls -R | Recursive listing: logs/ now holds both log files.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Moving house","md":"`cp` is photocopying a document and putting the copy in another room. `mv` is carrying the original to the other room (or writing a new label on it: renaming). `rm` is the shredder, and there is no bin to fish it out of. A wildcard is saying \"every box labelled *kitchen*\": convenient, until a box was mislabelled."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Safe file habits","md":"- **Back up before editing configs**: `cp nginx.conf nginx.conf.$(date +%F)` takes one second.\n- **Preview globs** with `ls` before `rm`, `mv` or `chmod` with the same pattern.\n- **Use `rm -i`** when unsure; it asks before each file.\n- **Keep configs in git** (you will, later) so every change has a history and an undo."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"File traps","md":"**`rm -rf` with a variable.** `rm -rf \"$DIR/\"` with an empty `$DIR` becomes `rm -rf /`. Scripts must guard against that.\n\n**Editing the wrong copy.** `.bak`, `.orig`, `.old` files sitting next to the real one. Check with `ls -l` and look at the full path.\n\n**Overwriting on copy.** `cp new.conf app.conf` silently replaces `app.conf`. `cp -i` asks first.\n\n**Spaces in names.** `rm my file.txt` deletes `my` and `file.txt`. Quote: `rm \"my file.txt\"`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Tidy up a project","md":"You inherited a messy project folder. Organise it, fix a config value with an editor, and clean up the junk without deleting anything that matters."}},"linux/linux-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"The full disk at 3 AM","md":"**03:07.** Your phone buzzes: `DISK CRITICAL: web2 / is 97% full`. A full disk is one of the most common outages there is: databases stop writing, apps cannot create temp files, logs stop, and deploys fail.\n\nYou are already logged in to **web2** as `ubuntu` (you have sudo). Free space safely, find out what filled it, and leave things better than you found them. Remember everything from this realm: measure first, understand before deleting, and keep the evidence."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What the Linux realm gave you","md":"- The shell and the filesystem tree; absolute and relative paths; `pwd`, `ls`, `cd`, Tab and history.\n- Files and editors: `mkdir -p`, `cp`, `mv`, `rm` (no undo), globs (preview first), `less`, `head`, `tail`, `nano`.\n- Users, groups and permission bits; `chmod`, `chown`, `sudo`, least privilege.\n- Streams and pipes: `grep`, `awk`, `sort`, `uniq -c`, `wc`, redirects, `tee`.\n- Processes and signals: `ps`, `top`, `kill` (TERM before KILL), exit codes, background jobs.\n- Packages and the environment: `apt`, `PATH`, `export`, `.bashrc`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"web2 is almost full","md":"Bring the disk under 80%. Things you may meet: huge logs, old backups, and a deleted file that still takes up space. Hint for that one: a running process may still hold a file open, so its space is not freed until the process lets go."}},"linux/packages-env":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"It works on my machine\"","md":"Jonas's deploy script worked perfectly on his laptop and failed on the build server with `jq: command not found`. He installed jq; next it failed with `API_URL: unbound variable`. On his laptop, `API_URL` was set in his `.bashrc` months ago and he had forgotten. On the server, nobody had set it.\n\nTwo invisible things make a command behave differently on two machines: **which software is installed** (and which version), and **which environment variables are set**. Making both explicit is the first step towards reproducible systems, and later towards containers, which package both."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Packages, PATH and environment variables","md":"**Packages** are software prepared for your distribution, installed with a **package manager**. On Ubuntu that is `apt`: it downloads packages from repositories, resolves dependencies, verifies signatures, and can update or remove them cleanly. Never download random binaries when a package exists.\n\nWhen you type `jq`, the shell searches the directories listed in the **PATH** variable, in order, for a file called `jq`. `which jq` shows which one it found. If none: `command not found`.\n\n**Environment variables** are key=value pairs every process inherits from its parent:\n\n| Variable | Typical use |\n|---|---|\n| `PATH` | where to look for commands |\n| `HOME`, `USER` | who and where you are |\n| `LANG` | language and character encoding |\n| `API_URL`, `DATABASE_URL`, `PORT` | configuration for your apps |\n\nApps read their configuration from the environment: the **Twelve-Factor** principle \"store config in the environment\" lets the same code run in development, staging and production with different settings.\n\n- `NAME=value` sets a shell variable, visible only in this shell.\n- `export NAME=value` makes it part of the environment, so programs you start inherit it.\n- `NAME=value command` sets it for that one command only.\n- Put permanent settings in `~/.bashrc` (for your interactive shells), then `source ~/.bashrc` to reload it now."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Installing and configuring","md":"```term\n$ sudo apt update                 # refresh the list of available packages\n$ sudo apt install -y jq tree     # install (-y: don't ask)\n$ apt list --installed | grep jq  # is it installed?\n$ which jq                        # where does the command come from?\n$ echo $PATH                      # the search path\n$ env | sort                      # the whole environment\n$ export API_URL=https://api.lumen.example.com\n$ PORT=8080 ./server.sh           # one-off setting for one command\n$ echo 'export PATH=\"$HOME/bin:$PATH\"' >> ~/.bashrc\n$ source ~/.bashrc                # apply it to the current shell\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The kitchen and the order slip","md":"Installed packages are the equipment in the kitchen: no blender, no smoothies, no matter how good the recipe. `PATH` is the list of cupboards the cook checks, in order, when the recipe says \"get the blender\". Environment variables are the order slip that comes with each dish: same recipe, but table 4 wants it without nuts and table 7 wants it extra spicy."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Making environments reproducible","md":"- **Write down dependencies**: a setup script, a package list, later a Dockerfile. Nothing should live only in someone's head or `.bashrc`.\n- **Same config mechanism everywhere**: environment variables (or files generated from them) in dev, CI and production.\n- **Pin versions** where behaviour matters, and update deliberately.\n- **Keep secrets out of `.bashrc`** and out of git; use a secret store (Security realm)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Environment traps","md":"**`sudo` resets the environment.** `sudo mycommand` may not see your exported variables or your custom PATH. Use `sudo -E` carefully, or pass what is needed explicitly.\n\n**Editing `.bashrc` and forgetting to reload.** Changes apply to new shells, or after `source ~/.bashrc`.\n\n**Overwriting PATH.** `export PATH=$HOME/bin` (without `:$PATH`) removes every other directory; suddenly `ls` is \"not found\".\n\n**Unquoted variables.** `cd $DIR` breaks if `DIR` contains a space. Quote: `cd \"$DIR\"`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Make the script work","md":"A teammate's health-check script fails on your laptop. Install what is missing, configure the environment it expects, and make your own scripts folder part of your PATH."}},"linux/pipes-text":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Who is hammering the login page?","md":"At 11:42 the login page slowed to a crawl. The access log had 1.2 million lines. Priya did not open it in an editor; she typed one line:\n\n```term\n$ grep 'POST /login' access.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -5\n```\n\nFour seconds later she had the answer: one IP address had sent 380,000 login attempts in an hour. A credential-stuffing attack. She blocked it at the firewall, and the page recovered.\n\nThat one line is five small programs, each doing one simple job, connected by pipes. Learning to build lines like that is one of the highest-value skills in operations."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Streams, pipes and redirects","md":"Every command has three **streams**:\n\n- **stdin** (0): input, from the keyboard or a pipe.\n- **stdout** (1): normal output.\n- **stderr** (2): errors and warnings.\n\n```diagram\npipes\n```\n\n| Syntax | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `a \\| b` | stdout of `a` becomes stdin of `b` |\n| `> file` | write stdout to a file (replace it) |\n| `>> file` | append stdout to a file |\n| `2> file` | write stderr to a file |\n| `> file 2>&1` | both stdout and stderr to the same file |\n| `2>/dev/null` | throw errors away |\n| `< file` | read stdin from a file |\n| `\\| tee file` | show output *and* save it |\n\nThe text toolbox:\n\n| Tool | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `grep` | keep lines matching a pattern (`-i` ignore case, `-v` invert, `-c` count, `-E` extended regex) |\n| `cut -d' ' -f1` | take fields by a delimiter |\n| `awk '{print $1, $9}'` | take columns, compute, filter (`$9 >= 500`) |\n| `sort` / `sort -n` / `sort -rn` | sort text / numbers / numbers descending |\n| `uniq -c` | collapse *adjacent* duplicates and count them (so sort first) |\n| `wc -l` | count lines |\n| `head` / `tail` | first / last lines |\n| `sed 's/old/new/g'` | replace text |\n| `tr a-z A-Z` | translate characters |"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Log analysis recipes","md":"```term\n$ grep -c ' 500 ' access.log                         # how many 500 errors?\n$ awk '$9 >= 500' access.log | wc -l                  # the same, by status column\n$ awk '{print $1}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head   # top IPs\n$ awk '{print $7}' access.log | sort -u | wc -l       # how many distinct paths\n$ grep -i error app.log | tail -n 20                  # the latest errors\n$ cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd                             # user names\n$ sed -i 's/timeout=5/timeout=30/' app.conf           # edit a file in place\n$ ./deploy.sh > deploy.log 2>&1                       # keep all output for later\n```\n\n```replay\ntitle: Building a pipeline step by step\nscenario: laptop\nsetup: printf '10.0.0.5 GET /\\n10.0.0.9 POST /login\\n10.0.0.5 POST /login\\n10.0.0.9 POST /login\\n10.0.0.9 POST /login\\n' > ~/mini.log\nstep: cat mini.log | Start by looking at the raw data.\nstep: grep login mini.log | Keep only the lines about /login.\nstep: grep login mini.log | awk '{print $1}' | Keep only the first column: the client IP.\nstep: grep login mini.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | Sort, so identical IPs sit next to each other...\nstep: grep login mini.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | ...because uniq -c only merges adjacent duplicates.\nstep: grep login mini.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | Biggest count first: 10.0.0.9 is the noisy one.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"An assembly line","md":"A pipeline is a factory line. Raw material (the log) enters at one end. The first station throws away the parts you do not need (`grep`), the next trims each part to size (`awk`), the next sorts them into order (`sort`), the next counts identical parts (`uniq -c`), and the last puts the biggest piles at the front (`sort -rn`). Each station is simple. The line is powerful."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Turning one-liners into knowledge","md":"- **Build pipelines incrementally**: add one stage at a time and look at the output.\n- **Save good ones** in the team runbook or as small scripts. Today's clever one-liner is tomorrow's on-call tool.\n- **Logs at scale** live in log platforms (you will meet them in the Observability realm), but the thinking is the same: filter, extract, group, count, sort."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Pipeline traps","md":"**`uniq` without `sort`.** It only merges *adjacent* lines, so unsorted input gives wrong counts.\n\n**`> file` when you meant `>>`.** One character, and yesterday's log is gone.\n\n**`sort` sorts text by default.** `10` comes before `9`. Use `sort -n` for numbers.\n\n**Redirecting into the file you read.** `sort data.txt > data.txt` empties the file before `sort` reads it. Write to a new file, then move it.\n\n**Errors vanish into pipes.** Only stdout flows through `|`; errors still print on the screen (or nowhere, if you redirected them)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Investigate the access log","md":"The login page was slow this morning. You have the web server's access log. Answer the team's questions with pipelines, and save a short report."}},"linux/processes":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The report that ate the server","md":"Every night at 2 a.m., a reporting script ran on Lumen's admin server. One night a change made it loop forever, pinning both CPUs at 100%. The admin site slowed to a crawl, and the on-call engineer got paged at 2:20.\n\nShe logged in, ran `top`, and saw it at once: `report.py` using 199% CPU for 18 minutes. She tried `kill` with its process ID; the script ignored the polite request because it was stuck in a tight loop without checking signals. `kill -9` ended it instantly. Then, instead of going back to sleep, she disabled the cron job so it would not start again at 2 a.m. tomorrow, and left a note for the team.\n\nFinding a process, understanding what it is doing, and stopping it safely are daily operations skills."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Processes, signals and exit codes","md":"A **process** is a running program. It has:\n\n- a **PID** (process ID) and a **parent** (PPID): processes form a tree, with `systemd` (PID 1) at the root;\n- an **owner** (the user it runs as), which limits what it can touch;\n- **resources**: CPU time, memory, open files, network sockets.\n\nYou talk to processes with **signals**:\n\n```diagram\nsignals\n```\n\nWhen a process ends, it returns an **exit code**: `0` means success, anything else means some kind of failure. The shell keeps the last one in `$?`, and `&&`, `||` and scripts make decisions with it.\n\n**Foreground and background**: a command normally runs in the foreground and holds your terminal. Add `&` to run it in the background, `jobs` lists background jobs, `nohup` keeps one running after you log out. (For real services you will use systemd instead, in the Services realm.)\n\n**Load average** (from `uptime` or `top`) is how many processes, on average, wanted CPU over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes. Compare it with the number of CPUs (`nproc`): a load of 2.0 on 2 CPUs is busy; on 16 CPUs it is idle."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Watching and controlling processes","md":"```term\n$ ps aux                          # every process: user, PID, %CPU, %MEM, command\n$ ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -5   # the five hungriest\n$ pgrep -a report                 # find PIDs by name\n$ top                             # live view (q quits); htop is friendlier\n$ uptime                          # load average\n$ kill 4312                       # SIGTERM: please stop\n$ kill -9 4312                    # SIGKILL: stop now (last resort)\n$ pkill -f report.py              # by name / command line\n$ sleep 300 &                     # run in the background\n$ jobs                            # background jobs in this shell\n$ echo $?                         # exit code of the last command\n$ false; echo $?                  # 1\n$ ls /nope && echo found || echo missing\n```\n\n```replay\ntitle: Exit codes drive decisions\nscenario: laptop\nstep: ls /etc/hostname; echo \"exit code: $?\" | Success: exit code 0.\nstep: ls /nope; echo \"exit code: $?\" | Failure: a non-zero exit code (2 for ls).\nstep: ls /etc/hostname && echo \"it exists\" | && runs the next command only after a success.\nstep: ls /nope 2>/dev/null || echo \"not there\" | || runs the next command only after a failure.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A busy kitchen","md":"Processes are cooks. Each has a name tag (PID), works for a department (user) and uses burners and counter space (CPU and memory). `ps` is the kitchen roster, `top` is looking around the room to see who is sweating. `kill` (TERM) is asking a cook to finish the current plate and leave; `kill -9` is security escorting them out mid-sauce, burner still on. The exit code is the cook's note on the way out: \"0, all done\" or \"1, something went wrong\"."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Process hygiene","md":"- **Measure before you kill**: note the PID, the user, the command line and how long it has run. That is your evidence for the follow-up.\n- **TERM first, KILL last.** Give the process a chance to clean up (close files, finish requests, release locks).\n- **Fix the cause.** Killing a runaway process is mitigation; the cron job, deploy or bug that started it is the real fix.\n- **Long-running things belong in systemd**, not in a forgotten `nohup` in someone's shell."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Process traps","md":"**Killing the wrong PID.** PIDs are reused. Re-check with `ps -p PID -o pid,user,cmd` right before you kill.\n\n**`kill -9` as a habit.** Databases and queues can corrupt data or lose messages when killed without cleanup.\n\n**`grep` finds itself.** `ps aux | grep report` lists the grep process too. Use `pgrep -a report`.\n\n**Load average without context.** A number means nothing without the CPU count."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"The runaway job","md":"Your laptop's fans are screaming and everything is slow. Find out what is eating the CPU, stop it properly, and practise background jobs and exit codes."}},"linux/shell-basics":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The server with no screen","md":"Nadia's first production incident at Lumen started with a message: \"web3 is slow, can you have a look?\" She opened the cloud console expecting a dashboard with buttons. Instead she got a black window with a blinking cursor: `ubuntu@web3:~$`.\n\nServers have no desktop, no mouse and no file explorer. Almost every server on the internet runs Linux, and you drive it by typing. That felt hostile for about a week. Then Nadia noticed she could do in one line what took twenty clicks, repeat it on fifty servers with a loop, and paste it into a runbook so anyone could do it at 3 a.m.\n\nThe terminal is not old-fashioned. It is the most precise, scriptable and shareable interface operations work has."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Shell, prompt, filesystem","md":"The **shell** (here: `bash`) is the program that reads what you type, runs it and shows the result. The **prompt** tells you where you are:\n\n```term\ndev@laptop:~/projects$\n```\n\n`dev` is the user, `laptop` the machine, `~/projects` the current directory (`~` means your home folder, `/home/dev`), and `$` means a normal user (`#` means root).\n\nEverything lives in one tree that starts at `/`, the **root**:\n\n```diagram\nfs-tree\n```\n\nA **path** names a place in that tree:\n\n- **Absolute** paths start at `/`: `/var/log/nginx/error.log` works from anywhere.\n- **Relative** paths start where you are: if you are in `/var/log`, then `nginx/error.log` is the same file.\n- `.` is the current directory, `..` is its parent, `~` is your home.\n\nA command line has a shape: `command -options arguments`, for example `ls -l /etc`. Options change *how* a command behaves; arguments say *what* it works on."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Moving around","md":"```term\n$ pwd                 # where am I?\n$ ls                  # what is here?\n$ ls -l               # long format: permissions, owner, size, date\n$ ls -la /etc         # everything in /etc, hidden files (starting with .) too\n$ cd /var/log         # go to an absolute path\n$ cd nginx            # go into a subfolder (relative path)\n$ cd ..               # up one level\n$ cd -                # back to the previous directory\n$ cd                  # home (same as cd ~)\n$ ls --help           # every command explains itself\n$ history             # what you typed before\n```\n\nTwo keys save hours: **Tab** completes commands and file names (press it twice to see the options), and **↑** brings back previous commands.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Walking the tree\nscenario: laptop\nstep: pwd | You start in your home folder, /home/dev.\nstep: cd /var/log | An absolute path: it works from anywhere.\nstep: pwd | The prompt and pwd agree: you are in /var/log.\nstep: cd .. | Two dots: the parent directory.\nstep: ls | cd .. took you from /var/log up to /var; ls shows what is in it.\nstep: cd ~ | Tilde: straight back home.\nstep: ls -la | -a shows hidden files (names starting with a dot), like .bashrc.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A building with corridors","md":"The filesystem is a building. `/` is the lobby, directories are corridors and rooms, files are the documents on the desks. An **absolute path** is a full address from the lobby: \"ground floor, east corridor, room 12\". A **relative path** is directions from where you stand: \"next door, then the second room\". `pwd` is checking the room number on the door; `cd ..` is stepping back into the corridor."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Habits of people who live in the terminal","md":"- **Look before you leap**: run `pwd` and `ls` before any command that changes things.\n- **Use Tab** constantly. It prevents typos and tells you whether a path exists.\n- **Prefer absolute paths in scripts and runbooks**: they work no matter where the reader is.\n- **Read `--help` and `man`** before asking: most answers are there, and they match the version you actually have."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Shell traps","md":"**Spaces in names.** `cd My Documents` tries to go to `My`. Quote it: `cd \"My Documents\"`, or press Tab.\n\n**Case matters.** `Readme.md` and `README.md` are different files on Linux.\n\n**Hidden files.** Configuration often lives in dot files (`.bashrc`, `.ssh/`). Plain `ls` does not show them; `ls -a` does.\n\n**The wrong machine.** Always read the prompt. `dev@laptop` and `ubuntu@prod-db-1` look similar at 3 a.m."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Find your way around","md":"Your laptop's filesystem looks like any Linux server's. Explore it: the system folders, the logs, your home folder and its hidden configuration."}},"linux/users-permissions":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"chmod 777, the universal \"fix\"","md":"Lumen's image upload broke after a deploy: `EACCES: permission denied, open '/srv/uploads/…'`. A tired engineer ran `sudo chmod -R 777 /srv` and uploads worked again. Ticket closed.\n\nThree months later, a vulnerability in an image library let an attacker write a file through the upload form. Normally that would have failed: the web app should only have been able to write into `/srv/uploads`. Because everything under `/srv` was writable by everyone, the attacker overwrote the app's own code. A one-line \"fix\" became a full compromise.\n\nPermissions are not an obstacle to work around. They are the walls between the parts of your system, and they decide how far an attacker or a bug can spread."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Users, groups and permission bits","md":"Every process runs as a **user**, and every file has an **owner** and a **group**. Users can belong to several groups. `root` (uid 0) is the administrator and bypasses permission checks.\n\nEach file carries three sets of three permissions:\n\n```diagram\npermissions\n```\n\n| Bit | On a file | On a directory |\n|---|---|---|\n| `r` read (4) | read its content | list the names inside |\n| `w` write (2) | change its content | create, rename, delete files inside |\n| `x` execute (1) | run it as a program | enter it (`cd`) and reach anything inside |\n\nLinux checks **owner** first, then **group**, then **others**: the first category that applies to you decides.\n\nNumbers add up per category: `7` = rwx, `6` = rw-, `5` = r-x, `4` = r--, `0` = ---. So:\n\n- `644` (rw-r--r--): normal files: owner edits, everyone reads.\n- `600` (rw-------): secrets, private keys: only the owner.\n- `755` (rwxr-xr-x): programs and directories everyone may use.\n- `750` (rwxr-x---): owner full, group can read/enter, others nothing.\n\n**sudo** runs a single command as root, if your user is allowed to (members of the `sudo` group on Ubuntu). It is safer than logging in as root: you only become root for one command, and it is logged."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Managing access","md":"```term\n$ whoami                                # who am I?\n$ id                                    # my uid, groups\n$ ls -l config.yml                      # read the permissions\n$ chmod 600 secrets.env                 # numbers…\n$ chmod u+x deploy.sh                   # …or symbols: u/g/o/a, +/-/=, r/w/x\n$ sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash deploy   # new user with a home folder\n$ sudo groupadd webteam                 # new group\n$ sudo usermod -aG webteam deploy       # add deploy to webteam (-a: append!)\n$ sudo chown deploy:webteam /srv/site   # change owner and group\n$ sudo chown -R deploy:webteam /srv/site  # recursively\n$ sudo -u deploy cat /srv/site/index.html # test access as another user\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"An office building with badges","md":"Every employee has a badge (user) and belongs to departments (groups). Each room has a sign: what the owner may do, what the department may do, what visitors may do. The facilities manager (root) has a master key. `sudo` is borrowing the master key for one door, while security writes down who borrowed it. `chmod 777` is propping every door open with a chair."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Least privilege in practice","md":"- **Every service gets its own user** (`www-data`, `postgres`, `lumen-api`), owning only what it needs.\n- **Secrets are `600`** and owned by the service user that reads them.\n- **Humans use their own accounts** with sudo, never a shared root password. Logs then show who did what.\n- **Group-based access** (`webteam` can deploy to `/srv/site`) scales better than adding individuals to files."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Permission traps","md":"**`chmod 777` as a fix.** It works by removing all protection. Find out *which* user needs *which* access, and grant exactly that.\n\n**`usermod -G` without `-a`.** It *replaces* all of the user's groups. You can remove someone (or yourself) from `sudo` by accident.\n\n**Forgetting directories need `x`.** A file can be `644`, but if a parent directory is `700` and owned by someone else, nobody else can reach it.\n\n**Group changes need a new login.** After `usermod -aG docker you`, your current shell still has the old groups. Log out and back in.\n\n**`sudo` with redirects.** `sudo echo x > /etc/file` fails: the redirect is done by *your* shell, not by sudo. Use `echo x | sudo tee /etc/file`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Set up a team folder","md":"The web team needs a shared folder for the site, a deploy user, and a secrets file that only that user can read. Set it up with the least privilege that works, and prove it."}},"network/firewalls":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"40,000 exposed databases","md":"Every year, security researchers scan the internet and find tens of thousands of Redis, MongoDB and Elasticsearch servers answering anyone who asks, many without a password. Most were not \"hacked\" in any clever way. Someone started a database on a cloud VM, it listened on all interfaces by default, and nothing stood between it and the internet.\n\nLumen nearly joined that list. A developer installed Redis on an app server for a quick cache experiment. A routine `nmap` from outside showed port 6379 open to the world. Nobody had decided that; nobody had blocked it either. The team made a rule that day: **every server starts with a default-deny firewall**, and every open port is a deliberate, documented decision."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Default deny, explicit allow","md":"A **firewall** decides which network traffic may reach a machine. On Ubuntu, `ufw` (uncomplicated firewall) is a friendly front end to the kernel's packet filter.\n\nThe safe model:\n\n1. **Default deny incoming**: nothing gets in unless allowed.\n2. **Allow exactly what the machine serves**: SSH for admins, 80/443 for a web server.\n3. **Restrict internal services by source**: the database port only from the app servers' addresses.\n4. Outgoing traffic is usually allowed (updates, APIs), though strict environments limit it too.\n\nIn the cloud you also have **security groups** (or network ACLs): firewalls in front of each VM, managed by the provider. Defense in depth means using both.\n\nWhat a blocked port looks like from outside is the key debugging clue:\n\n| What you see | Means |\n|---|---|\n| Connection refused | the packet arrived; nothing listens there |\n| Timed out / filtered | the packet was dropped on the way: a firewall |\n| Open | something listens and you are allowed in |"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Building a firewall without locking yourself out","md":"```term\n$ sudo ufw status verbose                # is it on? which rules?\n$ sudo ufw default deny incoming         # the safe default\n$ sudo ufw allow OpenSSH                 # FIRST: keep your way in\n$ sudo ufw allow 'Nginx Full'            # 80 and 443\n$ sudo ufw allow from 10.0.0.21 to any port 6379   # one client only\n$ sudo ufw enable                        # turn it on\n$ sudo ufw status numbered               # rules with numbers\n$ sudo ufw delete 3                      # remove rule number 3\n$ nmap -p 1-10000 app1                   # from OUTSIDE: what is really reachable?\n```\n\nThe order matters: allow SSH *before* `ufw enable` when you are connected over SSH. Otherwise the firewall drops your own session, and you need the cloud provider's console to get back in."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A nightclub door","md":"Default deny is a club with a guest list: if you are not on it, you do not get in, no matter how nicely you ask. Allow rules are names on the list (\"anyone for the main room, ports 80 and 443\"; \"only the staff from the kitchen for the storeroom, port 6379\"). A timeout is the bouncer ignoring you entirely. Forgetting to put yourself on the list before locking the door is how you end up calling the landlord (the cloud console) at midnight."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Firewalls in a team","md":"- **Rules as code**: firewall rules and security groups live in Terraform or Ansible (later realms), reviewed like any change.\n- **Scan yourself regularly** from outside the network. What you *think* is exposed and what *is* exposed drift apart.\n- **Prefer private networks** for internal traffic; then even a misconfigured service is not on the internet.\n- **Document every open port** with an owner and a reason."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Firewall traps","md":"**Locking yourself out** by enabling a default-deny firewall without an SSH rule.\n\n**\"It's behind the firewall, so no password needed.\"** Firewalls get misconfigured; services still need authentication.\n\n**Opening a port to `0.0.0.0/0` \"temporarily\".** Temporary rules are the ones nobody removes.\n\n**Testing from the server itself.** Local connections bypass the host firewall. Test from another machine."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Close the doors","md":"Server **app1** runs nginx (web), an internal app on port 3000 and Redis on 6379, with no firewall at all. From your laptop, see what is exposed, then lock app1 down so the public only reaches the website, and only the worker server can reach Redis. If you lock yourself out, `console app1` opens the cloud provider's emergency console."}},"network/http-curl":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The redirect loop that wasn't","md":"Monday morning, the mobile app showed \"too many redirects\" for half the users. The developers swore nothing had changed. Amira ran one command:\n\n```term\n$ curl -sI https://api.lumen.example.com/v2/events\nHTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently\nLocation: https://api.lumen.example.com/v2/events/\n```\n\nand then the same with the trailing slash, which redirected back without it. A new load balancer rule added slashes; the app removed them. Two components, each \"correct\", sending the client back and forth forever. Reading raw HTTP headers turned a two-day blame game into a ten-minute fix.\n\nHTTP is the language of almost everything you will operate: websites, APIs, health checks, webhooks, Kubernetes probes, cloud APIs. Being able to speak it directly with `curl` is a superpower."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Requests and responses","md":"A **request** has a method, a path, headers and an optional body:\n\n```term\nPOST /v1/tickets HTTP/1.1\nHost: api.lumen.example.com\nAuthorization: Bearer 7f3a…\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"event\": 42, \"seats\": 2}\n```\n\nA **response** has a status code, headers and a body:\n\n```term\nHTTP/1.1 201 Created\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"ticket\": \"T-9912\", \"seats\": 2}\n```\n\n**Methods** say what you want: `GET` reads, `POST` creates, `PUT`/`PATCH` update, `DELETE` removes. `HEAD` is GET without the body.\n\n**Status codes** say what happened:\n\n| Range | Meaning | Common ones |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2xx | Success | 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content |\n| 3xx | Go elsewhere | 301 Moved Permanently, 302 Found, 304 Not Modified |\n| 4xx | *Your* request is wrong | 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 429 Too Many Requests |\n| 5xx | *The server* failed | 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable, 504 Gateway Timeout |\n\n**Headers** carry metadata: `Content-Type`, `Location` (where a redirect points), `Authorization`, `Cache-Control`, `Set-Cookie`."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"curl, the HTTP Swiss army knife","md":"```term\n$ curl https://api.example.com/v1/events            # GET, print the body\n$ curl -i  URL                                      # include status line and headers\n$ curl -I  URL                                      # headers only (HEAD)\n$ curl -L  URL                                      # follow redirects\n$ curl -v  URL                                      # the whole conversation\n$ curl -s  URL | jq '.[0]'                          # quiet, pipe JSON into jq\n$ curl -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n       -d '{\"seats\":2}' URL                         # send a body\n$ curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" URL        # add a header\n$ curl -sf URL || echo \"down\"                       # -f: fail on 4xx/5xx (for scripts)\n$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\\n\" URL      # just the status code\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Ordering at a counter","md":"A request is your order: what you want (method), from which menu item (path), with notes (headers) and maybe a filled-in form (body). The response starts with a one-word verdict: \"here you go\" (200), \"it moved to the other counter\" (301), \"you didn't pay\" (401), \"we don't sell that\" (404), \"the kitchen is on fire\" (500). Read the verdict before you look at the plate."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"HTTP in daily operations","md":"- **Health endpoints** (`/health`, `/ready`) return 200 when the service can do its job. Load balancers, Kubernetes and monitors call them constantly.\n- **Status codes drive alerts**: a rising share of 5xx means the server side is failing; 4xx usually means clients or bad deploys of clients.\n- **`curl -v` is the first tool in any \"it doesn't work\" thread**: paste the exact request and response, not \"the API is broken\"."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"HTTP traps","md":"**Reading only the body.** An error page with status 200, or JSON with status 500: always check the status line (`-i`).\n\n**`curl -k` in scripts.** It disables certificate checks; fine for a one-off test, a security hole when left in automation.\n\n**Not following redirects.** Without `-L`, curl shows the 301 and stops; with `-L` in a loop, you will see redirect loops quickly.\n\n**Tokens in shell history.** `curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer abc123\"` lands in `~/.bash_history`. Read tokens from a file or variable."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Talk to the Lumen API","md":"The Lumen API lives at `https://api.lumen.example.com`. Use curl to read events, follow a redirect, and buy tickets, which needs the token stored in `~/api-token.txt`."}},"network/ip-ports-dns":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The migration that worked everywhere except for customers","md":"Lumen moved its API to a new server on a Tuesday evening. The team updated the DNS record, tested from their laptops, and saw the new server answer. Everyone went home.\n\nOvernight, a third of customers kept hitting the *old* server, which had been switched off. The DNS record had a **TTL of 24 hours**: resolvers around the world had cached the old address and would not ask again until the next day. The team's laptops had asked fresh. Customers' internet providers had not.\n\nThe fix next time was simple and boring: lower the TTL to 5 minutes a day *before* a migration, move, verify, then raise it again. Networking problems are rarely mysterious once you know where each piece of information lives and how long it is remembered."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Addresses, ports and names","md":"An **IP address** identifies a machine on a network: `10.0.0.12`, `203.0.113.7`. Some ranges are **private** (`10.x`, `172.16-31.x`, `192.168.x`): reachable only inside a network, like servers in a cloud VPC. Others are **public**, reachable from the internet.\n\nA **port** (0 to 65535) identifies a service on that machine. A program **listens** on a port; clients **connect** to address + port.\n\n| Port | Usually |\n|---|---|\n| 22 | SSH |\n| 80 / 443 | HTTP / HTTPS |\n| 5432 | PostgreSQL |\n| 6379 | Redis |\n| 3000, 8080 | app servers in development |\n\nWhere a program listens matters as much as the port:\n\n```diagram\nbind-address\n```\n\n**DNS** turns names into addresses. Your machine asks a resolver, which asks the authoritative servers for the domain, and caches the answer for the record's **TTL**. Common record types:\n\n| Record | Maps | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | name → IPv4 address | `api.lumen.example.com → 10.0.0.12` |\n| AAAA | name → IPv6 address | |\n| CNAME | name → another name | `www → lumen.example.com` |\n| MX | domain → mail servers | |\n| TXT | name → text (verification, SPF) | |\n\n`/etc/hosts` is checked *before* DNS: a local override, handy for testing and dangerous when forgotten.\n\nWhen a connection fails, the error tells you where:\n\n- **Could not resolve host**: DNS has no answer (wrong name, missing record).\n- **Connection refused**: the machine answered \"nothing listens on that port\" (or only on 127.0.0.1).\n- **Timed out**: nothing answered at all: usually a firewall dropping packets, or the host is down."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Looking at the network","md":"```term\n$ ip a                       # my addresses (inet 10.0.0.10/24 on eth0)\n$ ip route                   # where packets go by default\n$ sudo ss -tlnp              # listening TCP ports, with the owning process\n$ dig +short api.lumen.example.com   # name → address\n$ dig www.lumen.example.com          # full answer, including CNAMEs and TTL\n$ nslookup api.lumen.example.com     # the older tool, same idea\n$ cat /etc/hosts             # local overrides\n$ nc -zv db.lumen.internal 5432      # is that TCP port reachable?\n$ ping -c 3 web1             # does the host answer at all (if ICMP is allowed)?\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Apartment buildings and a phone book","md":"An IP address is a building's street address. A port is the apartment number inside: the building can be right there, but if nobody lives in apartment 5432, your knock goes unanswered (refused). A firewall is a doorman who silently turns you away without a word (timeout). DNS is the phone book that turns \"Lumen API\" into an address, and the TTL is how long people keep using the page they copied before checking the book again."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Network hygiene","md":"- **Bind databases and admin tools to private interfaces** (127.0.0.1 or a private IP), never to the public internet.\n- **Lower DNS TTLs before migrations**, then raise them again.\n- **Name things**: use DNS names in config, not IP addresses, so you can move services without editing every app.\n- **Document ports and flows**: which service talks to which, on which port. It is the map you need during incidents and security reviews."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Network traps","md":"**\"It works on my machine\" with `/etc/hosts`.** A forgotten override on your laptop hides a broken DNS record.\n\n**Testing from the server itself.** `curl localhost:3000` working proves the app runs; it says nothing about whether anyone *else* can reach it.\n\n**Confusing refused and timed out.** They point to different causes: a stopped service versus a firewall.\n\n**Hard-coded IPs.** They break silently when servers are replaced."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Map the network","md":"You are on your laptop, connected to Lumen's internal network. Find your own address and listening ports, query DNS, and test which services are reachable. Pay attention to *how* each failure fails."}},"network/network-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"The site is down","md":"**Friday, 17:40.** Lumen's events site, `https://events.lumen.example.com`, is down. Over the afternoon the team migrated it from the old server **web-old** to a new server **web3**, and \"it worked when we tested\". Customers disagree.\n\nSeveral things are wrong at once. That is normal in real incidents: fix one, and the next one appears. Work from the outside in: **name → network → port → process → configuration**, and confirm each fix from your laptop, the way customers see it.\n\nTools you have: `dig`, `curl`, `nc`, `ssh ubuntu@web3`, and the DNS provider's CLI, `dnsctl` (`dnsctl list`, `dnsctl set <name> A <ip>`)."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What the Networking realm gave you","md":"- IP addresses, ports, listening addresses (127.0.0.1 vs 0.0.0.0), DNS records, TTLs and `/etc/hosts`.\n- HTTP: methods, status codes, headers, redirects; `curl -i`, `-I`, `-L`, `-v`, `-w`.\n- SSH with keys, `~/.ssh/config`, `scp`, and the permission rules SSH enforces.\n- Firewalls: default deny, allow SSH first, refused versus timed out, scanning from outside.\n- TLS: certificates, CAs, expiry, names, certbot and automatic renewal."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Bring the events site back","md":"Fix every layer until `curl https://events.lumen.example.com/` returns **200** from your laptop, then write a short summary in `~/postmortem.md`."}},"network/ssh-keys":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The shared password","md":"When Lumen had five servers, everyone logged in as `root` with the same password, kept in a spreadsheet. Then a contractor left on bad terms. Changing the password meant updating five servers and telling twelve people. Two weeks later they found a cron job, added under root, that no one could attribute to anyone.\n\nThe team switched to **SSH keys**: each person has their own key pair, each server lists exactly which public keys may log in as which user, password login is disabled, and root login is off. When someone leaves, you remove one line. Every login is logged with the key that was used."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"How SSH keys work","md":"**SSH** gives you an encrypted shell on a remote machine. With key authentication:\n\n```diagram\nssh-keys\n```\n\n- `ssh-keygen` creates a **key pair**: a **private key** (`~/.ssh/id_ed25519`) that never leaves your machine, and a **public key** (`id_ed25519.pub`) you can share freely.\n- The server lists allowed public keys in `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` of the account you log into.\n- At login, the server sends a challenge only your private key can sign. No secret crosses the network.\n\nSSH is strict about permissions, because a key anyone can read is not private:\n\n| Path | Mode |\n|---|---|\n| `~/.ssh/` | `700` |\n| `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` (private key) | `600` |\n| `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` | `600` |\n\nThe first time you connect, SSH records the server's **host key** in `~/.ssh/known_hosts`. If it ever changes unexpectedly, SSH refuses loudly: it could be a reinstalled server, or someone in the middle.\n\n`~/.ssh/config` saves typing: aliases, users, key files and ports per host."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Everyday SSH","md":"```term\n$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"dev@lumen\"        # make a key pair (add a passphrase!)\n$ ssh-copy-id ubuntu@web1                     # install your public key (needs password login once)\n$ ssh ubuntu@web1                             # log in\n$ ssh ubuntu@web1 \"uptime && df -h /\"         # run one command and come back\n$ ssh -i ~/.ssh/deploy_key deploy@web1        # use a specific key\n$ ssh -v ubuntu@web1                          # verbose: see why authentication fails\n$ scp notes.md ubuntu@web1:/tmp/              # copy a file over SSH\n$ rsync -avz site/ ubuntu@web1:/srv/site/     # sync a folder efficiently\n$ exit                                        # leave the remote shell\n```\n\nAn entry in `~/.ssh/config`:\n\n```term\nHost prod-web\n    HostName web1\n    User ubuntu\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n```\n\nthen simply `ssh prod-web`."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A wax seal","md":"Your private key is your personal wax-seal stamp; the public key is a picture of the seal that you hand out. The server keeps the pictures of seals it accepts. To get in, you stamp a fresh piece of wax in front of the guard, who compares it with the picture. Nobody ever needs your stamp, and a lost picture is harmless. A lost stamp is not: that is why the private key is `600` and protected by a passphrase."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"SSH at scale","md":"- **One key per person** (and per laptop), never shared keys. Remove a person's key when they leave.\n- **No password logins, no root logins** on servers (`PasswordAuthentication no`, `PermitRootLogin no`).\n- **Bastion hosts** (jump boxes) or session managers for production access, with logging.\n- Bigger teams move to **SSH certificates** or identity-aware access, where short-lived credentials expire on their own."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"SSH traps","md":"**`UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE`.** Your key is readable by others: `chmod 600` it.\n\n**Wrong user.** `ssh web1` uses your local username; the server may expect `ubuntu`, `ec2-user` or `admin`.\n\n**Ignoring `REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED`.** Find out why before you `ssh-keygen -R` it away.\n\n**Copying the private key to a server** so you can \"hop\" further. Use `ssh -J` (jump) or agent forwarding carefully instead."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Keys, not passwords","md":"A new server, **web1**, still allows a password login for the initial setup. Create your key, install it, connect, make yourself an alias, copy a file, and fix a teammate's key that SSH refuses to use."}},"network/tls-https":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The certificate nobody owned","md":"On a Saturday morning, Lumen's checkout started failing for every customer: browsers showed a full-page warning, *Your connection is not private*. The TLS certificate had expired at midnight. It had been bought two years earlier by someone who had since left; the renewal reminders went to their old mailbox.\n\nExpired certificates are one of the most common causes of outages, at companies of every size. The fix that day took ten minutes. The lasting fix was to stop buying certificates by hand: free, automated certificates from Let's Encrypt, renewed by a timer every 60 days, and a monitor that alerts 14 days before any certificate expires."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What TLS gives you","md":"**TLS** (the S in HTTPS) provides two things:\n\n1. **Encryption**: nobody between client and server can read or change the traffic.\n2. **Identity**: the client can be sure it is talking to the real `shop.example.com`, not an impostor.\n\nIdentity comes from a **certificate**: a document saying \"this public key belongs to shop.example.com\", signed by a **certificate authority (CA)** your browser or OS already trusts.\n\n```diagram\ntls\n```\n\nA client rejects a certificate when it is:\n\n- **expired** (or not yet valid);\n- **for another name** (the name must match the certificate's subject or a SAN entry);\n- **self-signed** or signed by an unknown CA.\n\n**Let's Encrypt** issues free 90-day certificates automatically. Its tool `certbot` proves you control the domain (the HTTP-01 challenge: Let's Encrypt fetches a file from `http://yourdomain/.well-known/…`, so port 80 must be reachable), installs the certificate in nginx, and renews it on a timer.\n\nOnce HTTPS works, redirect all HTTP traffic to HTTPS (`301`), so nobody keeps using the insecure version."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Inspecting and fixing TLS","md":"```term\n$ curl -I https://shop.example.com                      # does it work?\n$ curl -v https://shop.example.com 2>&1 | grep -i expire\n$ openssl s_client -connect shop.example.com:443 -servername shop.example.com\n$ openssl x509 -in cert.pem -noout -dates -subject      # read a certificate file\n$ sudo certbot --nginx -d shop.example.com              # get + install a certificate\n$ sudo certbot certificates                             # what do we have, when does it expire?\n$ sudo certbot renew --dry-run                          # does renewal work?\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A passport at the border","md":"A certificate is a passport. The name on it must match the person (the domain), it must not be expired, and it must be issued by a country the border officer recognises (a trusted CA). A self-signed certificate is a passport you printed yourself: maybe the details are true, but no officer can trust it."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Certificates as a system, not a chore","md":"- **Automate issuance and renewal** (certbot, cert-manager in Kubernetes, managed certificates at the load balancer).\n- **Monitor expiry** independently of the renewal tool, with alerts weeks ahead.\n- **Own it**: certificates and DNS belong to a team, not to a person's mailbox.\n- **Redirect HTTP to HTTPS** and consider HSTS once everything works over HTTPS."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"TLS traps","md":"**`curl -k` \"fixes\" it.** It only stops curl from checking. Browsers and other clients still fail.\n\n**Blocking port 80 entirely.** It breaks the HTTP-01 challenge, so renewal fails silently weeks later.\n\n**Certificate for `www` only.** `example.com` and `www.example.com` are different names; include both.\n\n**Testing on the server.** Check from outside, with the real hostname, so SNI and DNS are exercised."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Saturday morning","md":"`https://shop.example.com` is down for every customer with a certificate error. The site runs nginx on **web1**; you can SSH in. Diagnose it, get a fresh Let's Encrypt certificate, and make sure it renews on its own."}},"scripting/bash-scripts":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The runbook that was really a script","md":"Lumen's on-call runbook for \"disk almost full\" had nine steps: check `df`, find the big folders, check the log sizes, compare with a threshold, and so on. Every on-call engineer ran them by hand, slightly differently, at 3 a.m.\n\nJonas turned the first five steps into a 20-line script, `check-disk.sh`. It printed the numbers in the same order every time, exited with code 1 when a disk was over the threshold, and so could be called from monitoring, from cron, or by a tired human. The runbook shrank to \"run `check-disk.sh`, then decide\". The best scripts start as exactly this: something you typed twice."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What a script is","md":"A **bash script** is a text file of commands, run top to bottom by bash. The same commands you type, plus variables, decisions and loops.\n\n```diagram\nscript-anatomy\n```\n\nThe pieces you need first:\n\n- **Shebang**: the first line, `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, tells the system which interpreter runs the file.\n- **Executable bit**: `chmod +x script.sh` lets you run it as `./script.sh`.\n- **Variables**: `name=value` (no spaces around `=`), used as `\"$name\"`. Always quote them.\n- **Arguments**: `$1`, `$2`… are the arguments, `$#` how many, `\"$@\"` all of them, `$0` the script's name.\n- **Defaults and required values**: `${1:-80}` uses 80 if `$1` is empty; `${1:?usage: …}` stops with a message if it is missing.\n- **Command substitution**: `used=$(df --output=pcent / | tail -1)` captures a command's output.\n- **Exit codes**: `exit 0` for success, non-zero for failure. Callers (cron, CI, monitoring, `&&`) decide based on it.\n- **Strict mode**: `set -euo pipefail` makes the script stop at the first failing command (`-e`), on unset variables (`-u`), and when any part of a pipeline fails (`pipefail`). It turns silent mistakes into loud ones."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Writing and running scripts","md":"```term\n$ nano ~/bin/hello.sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nname=\"${1:-world}\"\necho \"Hello, $name! You are $(whoami) on $(hostname).\"\n\n$ chmod +x ~/bin/hello.sh\n$ ~/bin/hello.sh            # Hello, world! …\n$ ~/bin/hello.sh Amira      # Hello, Amira! …\n$ bash -x ~/bin/hello.sh    # trace: print each command before running it\n$ echo $?                   # exit code of the last command\n```\n\nArithmetic and tests:\n\n```term\npct=$(df / | awk 'NR==2 {print $5}' | tr -d '%')\nif (( pct > 80 )); then\n  echo \"disk at ${pct}%\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A recipe card","md":"A script is a recipe card instead of cooking from memory. The shebang says which kitchen it is for. Arguments are the blanks (\"serves ___\"). `set -e` is the rule \"if a step fails, stop: do not serve a half-cooked dish\". The exit code is the note you leave for whoever comes next: \"done\" or \"something went wrong\"."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Scripts others can rely on","md":"- **Start every script with `set -euo pipefail`** (and know its quirks).\n- **Print a usage line** when arguments are missing.\n- **Exit codes are an API**: 0 only when it really worked.\n- **Keep scripts in git**, reviewed like code, with a comment on what they are for.\n- **Run `shellcheck`** (a linter for shell scripts) in CI; it catches most quoting bugs."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Script traps","md":"**Spaces around `=`.** `name = value` runs a command called `name`.\n\n**Unquoted variables.** `rm $file` with `file=\"my report.pdf\"` removes two files that don't exist, or worse.\n\n**Forgetting the exit code.** A script that prints \"ERROR\" but exits 0 looks green to cron and CI.\n\n**Windows line endings** in scripts edited elsewhere: `/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"check-disk.sh","md":"Write the disk check the on-call team has been doing by hand. Your script will be graded by running it, so its output and exit code both matter."}},"scripting/conditionals-loops":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Twelve tabs every morning","md":"Every morning, Priya opened twelve browser tabs to check that Lumen's sites and APIs were up: the shop, the API, the status page, the admin tools. It took ten minutes and she still missed things on days she was busy.\n\nHer script took an afternoon: read a list of URLs from a file, `curl` each one, print `OK` or `FAIL` with the status code, and exit non-zero if anything failed. It ran from cron every five minutes and posted failures to the team channel. Loops and conditions turn a checklist into a tool."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Decisions and repetition","md":"**Conditions** use exit codes: `if command; then …; fi` runs the block when the command succeeds. The test commands:\n\n| Test | True when |\n|---|---|\n| `[[ -f path ]]` / `[[ -d path ]]` | file / directory exists |\n| `[[ -z \"$s\" ]]` / `[[ -n \"$s\" ]]` | string empty / not empty |\n| `[[ \"$a\" == \"$b\" ]]`, `[[ \"$a\" != \"$b\" ]]` | strings equal / differ (`==` also matches globs: `== *.log`) |\n| `[[ \"$s\" =~ ^v[0-9]+ ]]` | regex match |\n| `(( n > 80 ))` | arithmetic comparison |\n| `[[ … ]] && [[ … ]]`, `\\|\\|`, `!` | combine, negate |\n\n**Loops**:\n\n```term\nfor host in web1 web2 web3; do          # over words\n  ssh \"$host\" uptime\ndone\n\nfor f in /var/log/app/*.log; do         # over files\n  gzip \"$f\"\ndone\n\nwhile read -r url; do                   # over lines of a file\n  curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} $url\\n\" \"$url\"\ndone < urls.txt\n\nfor ((i = 1; i <= 3; i++)); do          # counting\n  echo \"attempt $i\"\ndone\n```\n\n**case** picks between many patterns:\n\n```term\ncase \"$1\" in\n  start)   systemctl start app ;;\n  stop)    systemctl stop app ;;\n  *)       echo \"usage: $0 start|stop\"; exit 2 ;;\nesac\n```\n\n**Functions** name a piece of logic: `check() { …; return 1; }`, called like a command (`check \"$url\"`), with `local` variables."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Patterns you will reuse","md":"```term\nfailed=0\nwhile read -r url; do\n  [[ -z \"$url\" || \"$url\" == \\#* ]] && continue     # skip blanks and comments\n  code=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\" \"$url\" || true)\n  if [[ \"$code\" == 200 ]]; then\n    echo \"OK   $code $url\"\n  else\n    echo \"FAIL $code $url\"\n    failed=$((failed + 1))\n  fi\ndone < \"${1:-urls.txt}\"\n(( failed == 0 ))                                  # exit code: 0 only if all passed\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A doorman with a list","md":"A loop is a doorman going down the guest list one name at a time. A condition is the question he asks each guest (\"on the list? ID matches?\"). `continue` is \"skip this one, next please\"; `break` is \"we're full, close the door\". The exit code is the report to the manager at the end of the night."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Loops in production","md":"- **Make loops safe to interrupt and re-run** (see the next lesson).\n- **Collect failures, report at the end, and exit non-zero** instead of stopping at the first error, when checking many things.\n- **Timeouts on every network call** inside a loop (`curl -m 5`), or one dead host stalls the whole run.\n- **`read -r`** always, so backslashes in input survive."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Loop traps","md":"**`for line in $(cat file)`** splits on spaces, not lines. Use `while read -r line; do …; done < file`.\n\n**Forgetting `|| true`** with `set -e`: a failing command inside the loop kills the whole script, even when you meant to record the failure.\n\n**Unquoted `\"$@\"`.** Without quotes, arguments with spaces split apart.\n\n**`[ $x == y ]` with an empty `$x`** becomes `[ == y ]`: a syntax error. Use `[[ \"$x\" == y ]]`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"site-check.sh","md":"Write the script that replaces Priya's twelve tabs. It will be graded by running it against healthy and broken sites."}},"scripting/robust-scripts":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"rm -rf \"$STEAMROOT/\"*","md":"In 2015, a popular game launcher's update script contained the line `rm -rf \"$STEAMROOT/\"*`. On some machines `$STEAMROOT` came out empty, and the line became `rm -rf \"/\"*`: it deleted every file the user could write, including mounted backup drives.\n\nThat bug appears in scripts everywhere, now including scripts written by AI assistants, which happily produce `rm -rf $BUILD_DIR/*` without a second thought. The defences are simple and boring: refuse to run with empty variables, check inputs, make every step safe to repeat, and show what you would do before you do it."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Idempotent, guarded, observable","md":"A robust automation script is:\n\n1. **Guarded**: it checks its inputs and environment before touching anything.\n   - `set -u` turns unset variables into errors; `${DIR:?DIR must be set}` gives a clear message.\n   - Refuse dangerous values explicitly: `[[ \"$DIR\" == / ]] && exit 1`.\n2. **Idempotent**: running it twice leaves the system in the same state as running it once.\n   - `mkdir -p` instead of `mkdir`; `useradd` only `if ! id user`; append a config line only `if ! grep -q …`.\n   - This lets you re-run after a failure without fear, which is the foundation of every automation tool you will meet (Ansible, Terraform, Kubernetes).\n3. **Observable**: it says what it does (`log() { echo \"[$(date +%T)] $*\"; }`), and supports a **dry run** (`--dry-run` prints actions instead of doing them).\n4. **Clean on exit**: `trap cleanup EXIT` removes temp files whether the script succeeds, fails or is interrupted."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Building blocks","md":"```term\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nDRY_RUN=false\n[[ \"${1:-}\" == \"--dry-run\" ]] && DRY_RUN=true\n\nrun() {                       # print, and only execute when not a dry run\n  echo \"+ $*\"\n  if [[ \"$DRY_RUN\" == false ]]; then \"$@\"; fi\n}\n\nTARGET=\"${TARGET:?TARGET must be set}\"\n[[ \"$TARGET\" == \"/\" ]] && { echo \"refusing to touch /\" >&2; exit 1; }\n\nid deploy >/dev/null 2>&1 || run sudo useradd -m deploy          # only if missing\ngrep -q '^max_conn=' app.conf || run sh -c 'echo max_conn=100 >> app.conf'\n\ntmp=$(mktemp -d)\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$tmp\"' EXIT    # always clean up\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A pilot's checklist","md":"Pilots run the same checklist every flight, even after ten thousand flights. Before take-off they verify inputs (fuel, weather, flaps): guards. Each item is \"set to X\", not \"move up a notch\", so doing it twice is harmless: idempotent. They call out each action aloud: observable. A robust script is a checklist the computer runs for you."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Reviewing automation, including AI-written automation","md":"- **Review generated scripts line by line**, especially every `rm`, `chmod`, `>` and loop over files. Ask \"what if this variable is empty?\"\n- **Run new automation with `--dry-run` first**, then on staging, then in production.\n- **Lint with shellcheck** in CI; it flags `rm -rf \"$VAR/\"*` and unquoted variables.\n- **Prefer purpose-built tools** (Ansible, Terraform) once scripts grow beyond a page: they give you idempotency and dry runs for free."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Robustness traps","md":"**`rm -rf \"$DIR/\"*` without a guard.** Use `\"${DIR:?}\"` and reject `/`.\n\n**`set -e` surprises.** It does not stop on failures inside `if` conditions or before `&&`/`||`; it also stops on harmless non-zero exits like `grep` finding nothing. Add `|| true` where a non-zero result is expected.\n\n**Non-idempotent appends.** `echo line >> file` on every run adds the line again and again.\n\n**Trusting the AI's confidence.** \"This script safely cleans your build directory\" is a claim, not a test."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Review the AI's cleanup script","md":"Your teammate asked the AI assistant for a script that cleans the build directory and sets up the build user. It \"works on their machine\". Review it, make it safe and idempotent, and add a dry-run mode. It will be graded by running it, including with a missing variable."}},"scripting/scripting-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"One command to deploy","md":"Lumen still deploys the notes service by copying and typing the release steps by hand (you did it in the Services realm). Your task: turn those steps into **`deploy.sh`**, run from your laptop:\n\n```term\n$ ./deploy.sh 2.1.0\n```\n\nIt must copy the release to web1, unpack it into its own release folder, switch the `current` symlink, restart the service, check `/health` for the right version, and **roll back automatically** if the check fails, exiting non-zero so a pipeline would notice."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What the Scripting realm gave you","md":"- Scripts: shebang, `chmod +x`, variables, arguments, `$(…)`, exit codes, `set -euo pipefail`.\n- Decisions and loops: `[[ … ]]`, `(( … ))`, `if`, `case`, `for`, `while read -r`, functions.\n- Robustness: guards (`${VAR:?}`), idempotency, dry runs, `trap`, and reviewing AI-written scripts like any other code."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"deploy.sh","md":"The release tarballs are in `~/releases/`. Your key works for `ubuntu@web1`, which can use sudo. Your script will be run by the grader: once with a good release, once with a broken one."}},"services/deploy-releases":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Half a deploy","md":"Lumen used to deploy by running `git pull` inside the live app folder on the server, then restarting. One evening the pull stopped halfway when the network blipped: some files were new, some old, and the app crashed on start. Rolling back meant figuring out which files had changed, under pressure, with customers waiting.\n\nThe team switched to **release folders**: each version is unpacked into its own directory, configuration lives in a separate shared place, and a single symlink, `current`, decides which version runs. Deploying is \"unpack next to the old one, switch the link, restart\". Rolling back is \"switch the link back\". The half-deploy problem disappeared, because the live code is never edited in place."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Releases, shared config, one switch","md":"```diagram\nreleases-symlink\n```\n\n```term\n/opt/notes/\n├── releases/\n│   ├── 2.0.0/        ← previous version, untouched\n│   └── 2.1.0/        ← new version, unpacked complete\n├── shared/\n│   └── notes.env     ← secrets and settings, outside any release\n└── current -> releases/2.1.0\n```\n\n- The systemd unit runs `/opt/notes/current/server.js` with `WorkingDirectory=/opt/notes/current` and `EnvironmentFile=/opt/notes/shared/notes.env`.\n- `ln -sfn releases/2.1.0 current` repoints the link in one atomic step (`-n` treats an existing link as a file, so it is replaced rather than followed).\n- A **health check** after the restart decides whether the deploy worked; if not, you roll back the same way.\n- Keep a few old releases for rollback; delete older ones so the disk does not fill.\n\nThis is exactly what tools like Capistrano, Ansible roles and many CI pipelines do for you, and the same idea (immutable versions, one pointer) reappears with container images and Kubernetes rollouts."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"A manual release","md":"```term\n$ scp notes-2.1.0.tar.gz ubuntu@web1:/tmp/\n$ ssh ubuntu@web1\n$ sudo -u notes mkdir -p /opt/notes/releases/2.1.0\n$ sudo -u notes tar -xzf /tmp/notes-2.1.0.tar.gz -C /opt/notes/releases/2.1.0\n$ cd /opt/notes && sudo -u notes ln -sfn releases/2.1.0 current\n$ sudo systemctl restart notes\n$ curl -s localhost:4000/health          # which version is running?\n$ journalctl -u notes -n 20              # if it did not come up\n$ sudo -u notes ln -sfn releases/2.0.0 current && sudo systemctl restart notes   # rollback\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A train switch","md":"Two complete tracks lie side by side: the old route and the new one, both fully built. A single lever (the symlink) decides which track the train (your service) takes. You never rebuild a track while the train is on it, and if the new track has a problem, you pull the lever back."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Deploys people can trust","md":"- **Build once, deploy the same artifact everywhere** (a tarball, later a container image).\n- **Config outside the release** (`shared/`), secrets with mode 600.\n- **Automate the steps** into one script or pipeline job, with the health check and rollback built in.\n- **Know what is running**: `/health` returning the version makes every conversation shorter."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Deploy traps","md":"**`ln -sf` without `-n`.** If `current` is a link to a directory, `-sf` creates the new link *inside* the old release instead of replacing `current`.\n\n**Secrets inside the release folder.** They get overwritten by the next release, or copied into every tarball.\n\n**No health check.** \"The restart command succeeded\" only means systemd started the process, not that the app works.\n\n**Deleting the previous release right after switching.** Keep it until the new one has proven itself."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Ship 2.1.0, survive 2.2.0","md":"web1 runs notes **2.0.0** from a release folder. Deploy **2.1.0** properly, then try **2.2.0** (the team says it is \"ready\"), and roll back if it misbehaves. The release tarballs are in `~/releases/` on your laptop."}},"services/logs-cron":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The backup that never ran","md":"Lumen had a nightly database backup \"for years\". When they finally needed it, after a bad migration, the newest backup was 11 months old. The cron job had been added under a user who was later deleted; the script it called had moved; and its errors went to a local mailbox nobody read.\n\nScheduled jobs are deceptively simple to create and easy to forget. Logs are the same: easy to write, easy to let grow until they fill a disk. Both need the same discipline: decide where output goes, keep it bounded, and check that the thing actually happens."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Where logs live, how they stay small, how jobs run","md":"**Logs** come from two places on a modern Linux server:\n\n- the **systemd journal** (`journalctl`), for everything services print;\n- **files in `/var/log/`**, written by programs that manage their own logs (nginx, many apps).\n\n**logrotate** keeps log files bounded. Its config in `/etc/logrotate.d/` says, per set of files, how often to rotate, how many old copies to keep, whether to compress them, and how to handle programs that keep the file open:\n\n```term\n/var/log/notes/*.log {\n    daily\n    rotate 14\n    compress\n    delaycompress\n    missingok\n    notifempty\n    copytruncate\n}\n```\n\n`copytruncate` copies the log and empties the original in place, which works for apps that never reopen their log file. The alternative is to rotate and then signal the app to reopen (a `postrotate` script).\n\n**cron** runs commands on a schedule. Each crontab line has five time fields, then the command:\n\n```term\n# ┌ minute (0-59)\n# │ ┌ hour (0-23)\n# │ │ ┌ day of month (1-31)\n# │ │ │ ┌ month (1-12)\n# │ │ │ │ ┌ day of week (0-6, Sunday = 0)\n# │ │ │ │ │\n  30 2 * * *   /usr/local/bin/backup.sh >> /var/log/backup.log 2>&1\n  */5 * * * *  /usr/local/bin/healthcheck.sh\n```\n\nCron runs with a minimal environment (a short PATH, no `.bashrc`), so scripts must use absolute paths and redirect their output somewhere you will look. (Systemd **timers** are the modern alternative, with logs in the journal.)"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Logs and schedules","md":"```term\n$ journalctl -u notes -p err --since today      # only errors today\n$ journalctl --disk-usage                       # how big is the journal?\n$ ls -lh /var/log/notes/                        # file-based logs\n$ sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/notes      # dry run: what would happen?\n$ sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/notes      # force a rotation now\n$ crontab -e                                    # edit your schedule\n$ crontab -l                                    # list it\n$ sudo crontab -u notes -l                      # another user's\n$ grep CRON /var/log/syslog                     # did it run? (or journalctl -u cron)\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Newspapers and an alarm clock","md":"Logs are a daily newspaper delivered to your door. Without rotation, the pile grows until you can't open the door; with rotation, you keep the last two weeks, bundle older ones, and recycle the rest. Cron is an alarm clock: reliable, but it rings in an empty room unless you decide where the alarm goes and check that someone heard it."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Making scheduled work observable","md":"- **Every job writes somewhere visible**: a log file you rotate, the journal, or a monitoring check.\n- **Alert on missing runs**, not just failures: a \"dead man's switch\" check that complains when a backup has not reported in 26 hours.\n- **Test restores**, not just backups. A backup you have never restored is a hope.\n- **Rotation for every file-based log**, with the size budget written down."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Log and cron traps","md":"**Relative paths and PATH in cron.** `backup.sh` works in your shell and fails from cron.\n\n**Output to nowhere.** Without a redirect, cron mails output to a local mailbox nobody reads.\n\n**`% ` in crontab lines** means newline to cron; escape it (`\\%`) in date formats.\n\n**Rotating without telling the app.** The app keeps writing to the renamed file; use `copytruncate` or a reopen signal."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Bounded logs, reliable backups","md":"On web1, the notes app's log file is growing without limit and there is a backup script nobody schedules. Set up rotation, schedule the backup, and prove it runs."}},"services/nginx-proxy":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Port 3000 on the internet","md":"Lumen's first public API listened directly on port 3000, open to the world. Then they needed HTTPS, then a second app on the same server, then a rate limit for a scraper, then gzip, then a maintenance page during deploys. Each feature meant code in each app.\n\nThe fix was a **reverse proxy**: nginx on ports 80 and 443, terminating TLS, compressing, limiting and routing; apps listening only on `127.0.0.1`, focused on business logic. Today almost every web service you will operate sits behind a proxy or load balancer like this."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"How nginx routes a request","md":"```diagram\nreverse-proxy\n```\n\nnginx's configuration is blocks and directives ending in `;`:\n\n```term\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name notes.lumen.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:4000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;\n    }\n\n    location /static/ {\n        root /var/www/notes;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n1. nginx picks the **server** block by port and `server_name` (the `Host` header), falling back to the `default_server`.\n2. Inside it, the most specific **location** wins: `=` exact, then the longest prefix, then regular expressions.\n3. The location either serves files (`root`, `try_files`), redirects (`return 301 …`), or forwards to an app (`proxy_pass`).\n\nOn Ubuntu, site configs live in `/etc/nginx/sites-available/`, and are switched on with a **symlink** in `sites-enabled/`. Always test before reloading: `nginx -t` checks the syntax, and `systemctl reload nginx` applies it without dropping connections. A config with an error never takes effect if you test first.\n\nWhen the app behind a `proxy_pass` is down, nginx answers **502 Bad Gateway** and writes the reason to `/var/log/nginx/error.log`."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Working with nginx","md":"```term\n$ sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/notes\n$ sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/notes /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\n$ sudo rm /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default        # if you don't need the welcome page\n$ sudo nginx -t                                   # syntax check\n$ sudo systemctl reload nginx                     # apply without downtime\n$ curl -H \"Host: notes.lumen.example.com\" http://localhost/   # test a server block locally\n$ sudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log /var/log/nginx/error.log\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A hotel reception","md":"Guests (requests) arrive at one front desk (nginx on 80/443). The receptionist checks which hotel wing they want (server_name) and which department (location), then either hands them a brochure from the rack (static files), points them to the other entrance (redirect), or phones the department and relays the answer (proxy_pass). If the department doesn't pick up, the receptionist says so politely: 502."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Proxies in production","md":"- **Apps bind to 127.0.0.1** (or a private network); only the proxy is public.\n- **`nginx -t && systemctl reload nginx`** is one habit, every time.\n- **Config in git**, deployed by automation, so every change is reviewed and reversible.\n- At larger scale the same role is played by cloud load balancers and Kubernetes ingress controllers: same concepts, different config syntax."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"nginx traps","md":"**Missing semicolon.** `nginx -t` points at the line *after* it: `unexpected \"}\"`.\n\n**Editing `sites-available` without linking it.** The file exists, nginx never reads it.\n\n**`proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:4000/` vs without the slash.** With a URI part, the matched location prefix is replaced: `/api/x` can become `/x`.\n\n**Restarting instead of reloading.** Restart drops in-flight connections; reload swaps workers gracefully."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Put the notes API behind nginx","md":"The notes API runs on web1, on 127.0.0.1:4000. Publish it at `http://notes.lumen.example.com` through nginx, serve its static files directly, and practise reading a 502."}},"services/services-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Ship the shop","md":"Lumen is launching a small **merch shop** at `shop.lumen.example.com`. You get a fresh Ubuntu server, **shop1**, the app (a Node.js release tarball on your laptop) and a deadline of tonight.\n\nPut it into production the way a professional would: a dedicated user, a release folder with shared config, a systemd service that restarts on failure, nginx in front, HTTPS with an automatically renewing certificate, a firewall that exposes only what is needed, rotated logs and a scheduled backup."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What the Services realm gave you","md":"- **systemd**: unit files, `enable --now`, `daemon-reload`, `Restart=`, the journal.\n- **nginx**: server blocks, locations, `proxy_pass`, static files, `nginx -t`, reload, reading 502s.\n- **Releases**: release folders, shared config, an atomic `current` symlink, health checks and rollbacks.\n- **Logs and cron**: logrotate, crontab syntax, absolute paths, output you can see."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Launch night","md":"Work through the launch checklist on shop1. Your SSH key already works for `ubuntu@shop1`, and DNS for `shop.lumen.example.com` already points at it."}},"services/systemd-services":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The app that died when Samir went home","md":"Lumen's first internal tool, a small notes API, ran on a server because Samir had started it by hand: `node server.js &` in his SSH session. It worked for weeks. Then the server rebooted for security patches on a Sunday, and on Monday the tool was gone. The week after, it crashed at 2 a.m. on a bad request and stayed down until someone noticed at 9.\n\nNothing was wrong with the code. What was missing was a **supervisor**: something that starts the app at boot, restarts it when it crashes, runs it as a dedicated user, and keeps its logs in one place. On Linux, that is **systemd**."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Units, states and the journal","md":"**systemd** is PID 1: the first process, parent of everything else. It manages **units**; a **service unit** describes one long-running program:\n\n```diagram\nsystemd\n```\n\nA minimal unit file, `/etc/systemd/system/notes.service`:\n\n```term\n[Unit]\nDescription=Lumen notes API\nAfter=network.target\n\n[Service]\nUser=notes\nWorkingDirectory=/opt/notes\nEnvironment=PORT=4000\nExecStart=/usr/bin/node server.js\nRestart=always\nRestartSec=2\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\n```\n\n| Line | Means |\n|---|---|\n| `After=network.target` | start once networking is up |\n| `User=` | run as this user, not root |\n| `Environment=` / `EnvironmentFile=` | configuration for the app |\n| `ExecStart=` | the command, with an absolute path |\n| `Restart=always` / `on-failure` | what to do when it exits |\n| `WantedBy=multi-user.target` | what `enable` hooks it into at boot |\n\nTwo separate ideas trip people up:\n\n- **start/stop** changes what runs *now*.\n- **enable/disable** changes what starts *at boot*. `enable --now` does both.\n\nAfter editing a unit file, run `systemctl daemon-reload`, or systemd keeps using the old version.\n\nEverything a service prints to stdout and stderr goes to the **journal**, read with `journalctl`."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Running services","md":"```term\n$ sudo systemctl daemon-reload            # after creating or editing a unit\n$ sudo systemctl enable --now notes       # start now and at every boot\n$ systemctl status notes                  # state, PID, memory, last log lines\n$ sudo systemctl restart notes            # stop + start\n$ sudo systemctl reload nginx             # re-read config without stopping (if supported)\n$ systemctl is-active notes               # scriptable: prints active/inactive/failed\n$ systemctl --failed                      # everything broken\n$ journalctl -u notes -n 50               # last 50 log lines of one service\n$ journalctl -u notes -f                  # follow\n$ journalctl -u notes --since \"10 min ago\"\n$ systemctl cat notes                     # the unit file systemd is using\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A stage manager","md":"A theatre's stage manager does not act. They know every performer's cue, call them on stage at the right moment, send in the understudy when someone collapses, and keep the show log. Your app is a performer; systemd is the stage manager; the unit file is the cue sheet; the journal is the show log."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Services in a real team","md":"- **Every long-running process is a unit.** No `nohup`, no `screen` sessions, no \"started by hand\".\n- **One user per service**, with `/usr/sbin/nologin` as its shell, owning only its own files.\n- **Restart policies with limits**: `Restart=on-failure` plus systemd's start limit stops a crash loop from hammering a database.\n- **Unit files in version control** (or generated by configuration management), never edited live without a record."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"systemd traps","md":"**Forgetting `daemon-reload`.** You edit the file, restart, and nothing changes; `systemctl status` warns that the unit changed on disk.\n\n**Relative paths in `ExecStart`.** systemd does not use your shell's PATH or cwd; use absolute paths and `WorkingDirectory=`.\n\n**Running as root by default.** Without `User=`, the service runs as root.\n\n**`enable` without `start`** (or the reverse): it works now but not after a reboot, or after a reboot but not now."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Give the notes API a supervisor","md":"The notes API on **web1** is still run by hand. Turn it into a proper systemd service with its own user, automatic restarts and logs in the journal, and prove that it survives a crash."}}};
